Certainly! Reducing garbage collection spikes is crucial for maintaining smooth performance in a real-time application like a physics engine. Object pooling is a good approach to minimize memory allocation and deallocation, which can help reduce garbage collection pressure.

### Object Pooling

Object pooling involves reusing objects instead of creating and destroying them frequently. This can significantly reduce the number of allocations and deallocations, thereby reducing garbage collection overhead.

### Optimized Implementation

Here's an optimized version of your `PhysicsEngine` class using object pooling:

```javascript
class PhysicsEngine {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.objects = new Array(capacity).fill(null);
    this.activeCount = 0;
    this.freeList = [];
  }

  addObject(object) {
    if (this.activeCount < this.capacity) {
      if (this.freeList.length > 0) {
        const index = this.freeList.pop();
        this.objects[index] = object;
      } else {
        this.objects[this.activeCount] = object;
      }
      this.activeCount++;
    } else {
      console.error('Pool is full, cannot add more objects');
    }
  }

  update() {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.activeCount; i++) {
      const object = this.objects[i];
      // update object position and velocity
    }
  }

  removeObject(object) {
    const index = this.objects.indexOf(object);
    if (index !== -1) {
      this.objects[index] = null;
      this.freeList.push(index);
      this.activeCount--;
    }
  }
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Initialization**:
   - The `capacity` parameter defines the maximum number of objects that can be managed by the engine.
   - The `objects` array is pre-allocated to the specified capacity.
   - `activeCount` keeps track of the number of active objects.
   - `freeList` is used to store indices of objects that have been removed and can be reused.

