Your current implementation of the `InputHandler` class is a good start for a platform abstraction layer. However, there are several improvements and optimizations you can make to ensure that it is scalable and maintainable. Here are some suggestions:

### 1. Use Weak References for Event Listeners
Using weak references can help prevent memory leaks by allowing the garbage collector to clean up unused listeners.

### 2. Support Multiple Event Types
Ensure that your abstraction layer can handle different types of input events (e.g., touch, mouse, keyboard).

### 3. Implement Event Bubbling and Capturing
Support event bubbling and capturing to handle events in a hierarchical structure, similar to DOM event handling.

### 4. Use a More Robust Event Listener Management
Consider using a more robust method for managing event listeners, such as using a `Set` to store unique callbacks.

### 5. Add Documentation and Type Annotations
Add documentation and type annotations to make your code more understandable and maintainable.

### 6. Support Custom Events
Allow for the creation and handling of custom events.

### 7. Implement Throttling and Debouncing
Provide mechanisms for throttling and debouncing events to handle rapid input sequences efficiently.

### Example Implementation

Here's an enhanced version of your `InputHandler` class with these improvements:

```javascript
class InputHandler {
  constructor() {
    this.listeners = new Map();
  }

  addListener(eventType, callback) {
    if (!this.listeners.has(eventType)) {
      this.listeners.set(eventType, new Set());
    }
    this.listeners.get(eventType).add(callback);
  }

  removeListener(eventType, callback) {
    if (this.listeners.has(eventType)) {
      this.listeners.get(eventType).delete(callback);
    }
  }

  emit(eventType, data) {
    if (this.listeners.has(eventType)) {
      this.listeners.get(eventType).forEach((callback) => {
        callback(data);
      });
    }
  }

  // Example of custom event handling
  addCustomEvent(eventName, handler) {
    this.addListener(eventName, handler);
  }

  // Example of throttling and debouncing
  throttle(fn, delay) {
    let lastCall = 0;
    return (...args) => {
      const now = Date.now();
      if (now - lastCall >= delay) {
        fn(...args);
        lastCall = now;
      }
    };
  }

  debounce(fn, delay) {
    let timeoutId;
    return (...args) => {
      clearTimeout(timeoutId);
      timeoutId = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
    };
  }
}

// Example usage
const inputHandler = new InputHandler();

inputHandler.addListener('touchstart', (data) => {
  console.log('Touch start:', data);
});

inputHandler.addListener('mousedown', (data) => {
